WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:04.290
It's almost impossible to end a hit show in
a way that makes everyone happy.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:08.900
But thanks to rushed execution, frustrating
character choices, and other inconsistencies,

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:13.299
a number of diehard Game of Thrones fans are
pretty annoyed with how everything shook out.

00:00:13.299 --> 00:00:17.199
Here's a spoiler-filled examination of what
we think went wrong with the final season

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:18.200
of Game of Thrones.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.720
For the first five seasons, showrunners David
Benioff and D.B. Weiss were able to mostly

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.869
follow Martin's books as a direct template,
and many of the show's most beloved and infamous

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:31.890
moments, including the execution of Ned Stark,
and the Red and Purple Weddings, were directly

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.120
out of the source material.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:37.700
Benioff and Weiss did make some welcome adjustments;
in the fifth book, Tyrion wanders around for

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:42.330
what feels like years before meeting Daenerys,
whereas in the show, they team up pretty quickly,

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.640
but for the most part, they stuck to the literal
script in front of them.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:49.860
However, once the show's fifth season ended,
so did the source material; the next book

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.800
in Martin's series, The Winds of Winter, is
still unfinished.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.720
And once Benioff and Weiss ran out of source
material, that's around the same time many

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:01.390
fans started complaining about being unsatisfied
with the handling of the show's overall story

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:05.990
and writing, even though Martin himself guided
them through the final acts of the series.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:09.440
Obviously Benioff and Weiss aren't without
talent, since they built the show fans grew

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:09.940
to love.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:14.700
But Martin is naturally better at manipulating
his own story, and problems began to arise

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.700
when the showrunners were left to their own
devices.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.060
It's understandable that, as Game of Thrones
went on and became a bigger and bigger hit,

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.740
HBO would want it to continue as long as possible.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:28.200
But in 2015, Benioff and Weiss had a public
dispute with the network over how long it

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.180
would run, expressing a desire to close out
the show after just seven seasons.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:36.800
Even though Martin himself said the show should
be at least 13 seasons, the showrunners and

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.860
HBO eventually forged a deal for an eight-season
run that would abbreviate seasons seven and

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:46.300
eight; the former would last for seven episodes,
while season eight would only consist of six.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:50.400
Many fans critical of the show have come to
proclaim that cramming years' worth of buildup

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:55.280
and anticipation into a shorter set of episodes
created a new set of problems, making it all

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.720
the more baffling that the showrunners insisted
on these short seasons.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:03.040
Without room to let the story breathe, the
final batch of episodes felt really rushed.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.460
As a result, they stand in stark contrast
to the show's earlier seasons that focused

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.640
on epic journeys, character development, and
long throughlines.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.660
As the eighth season began with two slower
episodes that harkened back to the show's

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:19.370
past, viewers got excited, but then it started
barreling towards its grand finale, letting

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:22.269
narrative threads come up short in confusing
ways.

00:02:22.269 --> 00:02:26.270
Characters jumped in and out of the story
at breakneck speeds, like Bronn's sudden appearance

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:29.020
and disappearance from episode 4 being just
one example.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:33.439
Seemingly insurmountable obstacles were overcome
in the blink of an eye, like the long-awaited

00:02:33.439 --> 00:02:34.650
war with the Night King.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.701
And characters seemingly teleported across
the map of Westeros when most of the early

00:02:38.701 --> 00:02:42.280
seasons of the show were literally about how
long it took to get anywhere.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:46.340
Fans knew Game of Thrones had to end eventually,
but no one wanted it to feel like it was trying

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.920
to get there as fast as possible.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:52.269
With so much to wrap up, it's ultimately disappointing
that the showrunners let their characters

00:02:52.269 --> 00:02:54.580
and plots suffer on the road to the finish
line.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:59.060
The biggest and most unfortunate side effect
of the rushed pace of Game of Thrones' final

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:02.880
season is that while some of the plots and
developments were exciting and welcome, their

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.820
execution left something to be desired.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.760
While there were plenty of issues to tackle,
Daenerys' battle strategies, Missandei's exit,

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.860
Euron's temporarily impeccable aim, Cersei's
maybe-real pregnancy, or how Bran became king

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:19.410
after doing basically nothing for the entire
season, the worst example is Daenerys' sudden

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:20.820
pivot into madness.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:25.480
After seasons of barbecuing her enemies and
conquering lands beyond Westeros, fans were

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:29.540
anxious to see if Daenerys would follow in
her father Aerys' footsteps and become the

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:34.870
In the end, it makes sense that she did; audiences
have been tracking Daenerys' vengeful, unhinged

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:36.499
impulses right from the beginning.

00:03:36.499 --> 00:03:40.889
But the only clear buildup viewers got for
Daenerys' full-on descent into insanity was

00:03:40.889 --> 00:03:43.290
her enraged reaction to Missandei's death.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.640
For a character who was always committed to
justice, some felt it was unearned to let

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.360
her torch thousands of innocents in King's
Landing.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:53.969
Instead of letting Daenerys experience a complex
and fascinating character arc as she succumbs

00:03:53.969 --> 00:03:58.150
to the madness she once so feared, the show
did her slow, steady growth to that point

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:03.180
a disservice, and arguably made a mockery
of one of its best and most interesting characters.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.700
Daenerys' quick heel turn to Mad Queen is
certainly one of the most obvious examples

00:04:07.709 --> 00:04:11.379
of season eight's uneven character development,
but she's not the only character to suffer

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:12.700
during the final season.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:16.090
If you want to know where season eight went
wrong with its characters, look no further

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.180
than Jaime Lannister.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:21.060
Jaime started Game of Thrones off in a pretty
bad spot, morally speaking.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:24.880
Y'know, trying to kill a little kid because
he caught you having sex with your twin sister

00:04:24.889 --> 00:04:26.289
is definitely frowned upon.

00:04:26.289 --> 00:04:27.620
"The things I do for love."

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:33.639
But over the course of the show, he became
a fan favorite, thanks to a redeeming character

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:37.220
arc and an utterly charming performance from
Nikolaj Coster-Waldau.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:41.460
Most of Jaime's redemptive streak was thanks
to his friendship and eventual tryst with

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.580
Brienne of Tarth, who loved the man beneath
the armor and helped him escape the clutches

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.440
of his conniving, cruel twin sister.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:51.830
However, after sleeping with Brienne, Jaime
deserts Winterfell, which he risked his life

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:55.520
to save in the battle against the Army of
the Dead, to return to his sister's side.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:59.140
So why's he suddenly leaving to go be with
his supervillain lover?

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:00.140
Jaime's only explanation:

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:05.840
"She's hateful, and so am I."

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:10.220
Some fans got their hopes up that he might
actually be returning to King's Landing to

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:11.240
kill his sister.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:15.280
But in the end, Jaime and Cersei reunited
just to die in each other's arms.

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:19.360
With this one move, it's not hard to argue
that Benioff and Weiss undid years of character

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:23.520
development, giving one of the show's biggest
characters one of the least satisfying conclusions

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:24.320
they could have chosen.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:27.840
And let's not forget about the other half
of this pair of doomed dummies.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:32.620
Cersei, one of the show's most fascinating
and twisted characters and ostensibly one

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:36.510
of the biggest threats to the show's heroes,
spent much of season eight drinking wine and

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:41.480
idly staring off a balcony, only to suffer
one of the series' most anticlimactic deaths.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:46.220
It may make a certain kind of sense for she
and Jaime to be reunited in their last moments...but

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:50.169
seeing the show's most cunning and cutthroat
villains get taken down by a pile of falling

00:05:50.169 --> 00:05:52.360
rocks is just plain unsatisfying.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.120
And this points to another big issue with
the show's final season: failing to pay off

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:57.780
on the series' many years of buildup.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.720
One of the major strengths of Game of Thrones
was always its effective tension-building,

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:05.840
and fans were excited for huge storylines
to come to a head in the show's final season.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:10.000
Make no mistake, season eight had plenty of
drama, but there were still several plots

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.180
that ended up with some pretty lackluster
payoffs.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:16.680
Another obvious example is the Night King,
built up throughout the show's run as a formidable

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:20.200
villain who can raise entire armies of the
dead with a flick of his wrist.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:24.200
Seeing Arya swoop in and take him down might
have been extremely fun in the moment, but

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:28.379
in the end, the entire narrative of the Night
King and the true history of the White Walkers

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:30.139
was left largely unexplained.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:35.440
Meanwhile, the show spent seven seasons building
to the revelation that Jon was a secret Targaryen,

00:06:35.449 --> 00:06:39.090
but threw it all out in the series finale,
giving the throne to a boy with no claim to

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:40.120
it whatsoever.

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:44.240
Season eight had some satisfying moments,
but ultimately, it suffered from a huge amount

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:46.140
of buildup that simply didn't pay off.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.720
On the subject of lots of buildup without
much payoff, right from the beginning, Game

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:55.220
of Thrones was rife with prophecies, focusing
on everything from world saviors to poisoned

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:56.740
wombs and fateful deaths.

00:06:56.780 --> 00:07:00.920
Since some of the prophecies came true during
the show's run, like the prediction made to

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:04.800
Cersei regarding her marriage and children,
it stood to reason that the rest of the prophecies

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:07.360
introduced would end up being extremely important.

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:09.220
Turns out, this wasn't the case at all.

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:13.400
With most of the show's prophecies abandoned
by the final season, it's hard to understand

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:14.770
why they were ever introduced.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:18.620
"Stannis was not the Prince who was Promised,
but someone has to be."

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:25.919
One of the earliest prophecies made in the
series concerns Daenerys and her child-bearing

00:07:25.919 --> 00:07:30.300
capabilities, which is made by a vengeful
witch who tells Daenerys that, more or less,

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.360
she will never be able to produce any heirs.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:36.660
Daenerys fully believes this, telling Jon
as much and treating her dragons as her children,

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:41.440
but in the end, Daenerys' fertility had as
much importance as Ed Sheeran's bizarre cameo.

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:45.599
Daenerys was also a possible contender for
the seemingly all-important theories about

00:07:45.599 --> 00:07:50.279
Azor Ahai and the Prince or Princess who was
Promised, but in the end, those prophecies

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:51.720
also fizzled into nothing.

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:55.560
Again, it makes sense that the series would
make some cuts to the narrative as it honed

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:59.639
in on its final episodes, but after seasons
of repeating these prophecies, seeing them

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:01.640
come to nothing is a disappointment.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:06.020
Along with moments that ended up happening
offscreen, plenty of plots from the previous

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:08.040
seasons ended up on the cutting room floor.

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:11.920
While not completing every single narrative
thread on Game of Thrones is understandable,

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:16.069
it feels wrong that so many huge developments
ended up having little to no bearing on the

00:08:16.069 --> 00:08:17.340
show's eventual conclusion.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:21.260
For example, Jon Snow went through quite the
journey throughout the series, becoming a

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:25.710
strong and honorable man who literally died
and came back to life for his people, all

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:29.400
before realizing his whole identity was a
lie told to protect his life.

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:33.800
But in the end, none of that mattered at all,
since the show shunted Jon back into the Night's

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:38.230
Watch without letting most of Westeros find
out there was another heir to the throne.

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:42.480
Arya spent two seasons training with the Faceless
Men, and while she might have picked up the

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:46.350
stealthiness and weapons training that allowed
her to take down the Night King, it seems

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:49.420
odd that she didn't face-swap once during
the final season.

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:54.020
These examples are just scratching the surface;
after placing so much emphasis on the religions

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:58.560
of Westeros in previous seasons, from the
Red Priests and Priestesses to the Old Gods

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.440
and the New, those were all basically thrown
out and forgotten by the show's end.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.660
Some plots were likely sacrificed for a reason,
but this many feels kind of ridiculous.

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:11.280
It would be impossible to include every single
character that ever appeared on Thrones in

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:16.120
the final season, but it's frustrating that
a few big names were left out entirely, especially

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:20.660
when comparatively new and altogether annoying
characters like Euron Greyjoy were allotted

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.180
plenty of screentime.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:25.360
"I'm the man who killed Jaime Lannister."

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:27.640
Shut up, you idiot!

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:32.600
Anyway, considering how important Bran turned
out to be in Game of Thrones' final episodes,

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:36.160
it feels really wrong that Meera Reed, the
faithful companion who dragged him through

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:39.760
the far North and saved his life several times,
never showed up again.

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:44.500
Other faces entirely absent from the final
season are Daario Naharis, Daenerys' loyal

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:49.790
mercenary and onetime boyfriend, and Arya's
assassin mentor Jaqen H'Ghar, both of whom

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:51.920
seemed to have unfinished stories left to
tell.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:56.580
And after a chance meeting in season seven,
Arya's rogue wolf Nymeria is never relevant

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:57.080
again.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:01.000
We'd have traded any of Euron's scenes for
some of these forgotten favorites to show

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:01.500
up again.

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:04.120
"I'm going to put a prince in your belly!"

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:06.520
No, seriously dude, shut up.

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:11.600
One of the biggest questions of the final
season was who would actually rule over Westeros.

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:15.100
Ultimately, the question of who would physically
take the Throne was tossed in the garbage

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:19.120
when Drogon, after finding Daenerys dead,
torched the chair out of existence.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:22.920
But the fact remained that the Seven Kingdoms
still needed a leader, and soon.

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:27.590
The Seven Kingdoms didn't exactly end up with
a leader, the Six Kingdoms did, after Sansa

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:30.470
demanded independence for the North and became
its queen.

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:34.430
The new king of the rest of the kingdoms ended
up being none other than Bran Stark, who seemed

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:36.860
to already have a full-time job staring into
space.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:41.940
As with Daenerys' descent into madness, Bran
becoming king makes some sense in the context

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:42.900
of the show overall.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:46.320
But to many fans, how it arrived there feels
basically absurd.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:50.460
Tyrion argues that Bran should be made king
because he had the "best story" of all.

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:54.220
But let's remember that this character is
sitting between his two sisters with way more

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:56.980
interesting stories and actual leadership
skills.

00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:01.730
Sansa became the badass Ice Queen in the North,
while Arya transformed into a super-assassin

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:03.620
and literally saved the entire world.

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:07.540
But sure, let's give the crown to the kid
with mind control powers who just sat there

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:10.080
and did nothing to beat his mortal enemy,
the Night King.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:14.280
At least they didn't give their new king a
really insulting nickname or anything, right?

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:16.320
"All hail Bran the Broken...All hail, Bran
the Broken!"

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:27.760
Ummm, well...never mind.

